    "case_id": "16",
    "case_summary": "A 69-year-old woman presented 11 years ago with intermittent mild to moderate pain on the right side of her abdomen, described as a dull ache, accompanied by nausea, bloating, and flatulence. She also experienced constipation, with partial symptom relief following defecation. Symptoms worsened after heavy meals, and she occasionally had steatorrhea. Physical examination showed mild right-sided abdominal tenderness. She had no other health issues and no history of abdominal surgery. Initial investigations, including an abdominal ultrasound scan (USS), a hepatobiliary iminodiacetic acid (HIDA) scan, and an oesophago-gastro-duodenoscopy (OGD), showed no abnormalities. Flexible sigmoidoscopy and colonoscopy were also unremarkable. She was advised to take laxatives regularly, which provided partial and temporary relief. Her symptoms persisted, and almost two years later, she underwent an endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP), a cream absorption test, a wheat intolerance test, and a CT scan of her abdomen, all of which were normal. She was advised to take antispasmodics, various combinations of laxatives, and a course of Pancrelipase (Creon) tablets, which provided only temporary relief. A gastroenterology consultant recommended another abdominal USS, OGD, and a barium follow-through study, all of which were normal. She continued to receive symptomatic treatment.\n\nHistopathological analysis revealed a densely crowded spindle and polygonal cell tumour showing moderate nuclear pleomorphism. No mitoses were found and the tumour spread into the mucosa and outwards into the serosa. There was no lymph nodal spread. Immunohistochemistry demonstrated positive staining for CD117 (cKit), and negative staining for broad range cytokeratins (MNF 116), CD34, desmin, S-100 and chromogranin.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
